The code statements that lead to the bug are:
acrtc_state = to_dm_crtc_state(connector->base.state->crtc->state);
r = copy_from_user(data, buf, write_size);
Reason:
The first statement dereferences connector->base.state and state->crtc without checking for NULL, causing a NULL pointer dereference when the connector is not bound to a CRTC (e.g., after hot-plug). The second statement uses a hardcoded write_size of 36 bytes regardless of the user-supplied size, leading to a buffer over-read when the user provides less than 36 bytes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!connector->base.state || !connector->base.state->crtc)
    return -ENODEV;
write_size = min_t(size_t, size, sizeof(data));
Reason:
The NULL check prevents dereferencing null pointers by returning an error code (-ENODEV) when the CRTC state is unavailable. The min_t() clamps the copy size to the user-supplied size or the buffer size, whichever is smaller, preventing a buffer over-read when the user provides fewer bytes than expected.

